# exponentiation_two:
# CSES 1712, Exponentiation II (https://cses.fi/problemset/task/1712), in our own
# words: three numbers a, b and c; a to the power (b to the power c), modulo 10^9 + 7,
# where a number to the power 0 is 1 (0^0 = 1 too). The input is three binary naturals
# (corpora/library/binary/natural) and the answer a binary natural. Part of the CSES
# port's fourth batch.
#
# THE PROGRAM, written by the definition: b to the power c is the product of c copies
# of b (c multiplications, the product of no copies being 1), and a to the power of
# that is the product of that many copies of a, all done on exact binary naturals and
# the product reduced modulo 10^9 + 7 at the end, with the same recursion for both
# powers. Its reading multiplies c times and then b^c times, numbers of up to b^c log
# a bits; the replacement the family asks for is the Fermat reduction, which finds b^c
# modulo 10^9 + 6 by fast exponentiation and then a to that by fast exponentiation,
# where 10^9 + 7 is prime and does not divide a, a multiple of it and b^c = 0 being
# handled apart, on fixed-width integers.
#
# DEFINITION 14: the program P, the point's term, is built of λ and application only:
# its text holds no top, no choice, no parallel and no literal. It β-reduces to V, the
# canonical binary numeral of the answer: every recursion ends, since power consumes
# its exponent, a binary numeral that falls by one at each call. binary_subtract,
# which writes no top (../common/binary_modular), is asked for exponent - 1 only for a
# nonzero exponent; the branches that a Scott boolean discards are never read. So ⊢_β
# P = V, and P ≃_O V by the soundness of the derivable equations for the contextual
# preorder of Definition 14 (Lean
# `ContextualPreorder.contextualEquivalenceOfDerivableLatticeEta`): V is an answer, P
# ⊑_O V. No other closed βη normal form W has P ⊑_O W: then V ⊑_O W, while Böhm's
# theorem gives a pure context that takes V to λx y. x and W to λx y. y, and applying
# both to λa. a and Ω makes the first solvable and the second not.
